  "notes": "The Hitmen's \"grimey one\", and this model is the label's own inverse. Where D-Dot and Stevie J get wide claps, four perc lanes and complete pop progressions, this one is \"engineer-turned-producer, so drums are tightly gated and dry\" with \"less percussion layering than the label norm\" — two perc lanes at [0,1] and a -9 dB offset, no clap layer, no rimshot, a 0.08 ghost probability and `hihat.fillDensity` at 0.28. His stated goal was to rock the streets rather than radio, so the keys go low and the material is string and horror-score-adjacent: harmonic minor and Phrygian sit above natural minor and the melody register bottoms out at 59. \"Almost no sweeteners\" is `motifPitchCount` [2,4] on a 2-bar loop. ⚠ **The bass is `mirror_kick`, not a held note, and that is an engine limit rather than a reading of the record.** The research says sustained sub plus sample bass on the tonic; `reese_sustain` emits one note per chord and saturates, so `mirror_kick` at `followRootsProb` 0.96 over a kick playing 1 and 3& is the nearest honest thing, with the register dropped to [22,40] for the \"heavy low-mid\". ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high: the philosophy is first-person, the drum numbers are not. The specific inferred parameter is `drums.percs.gainOffsetDb` — \"less percussion layering than the label norm\" is comparative, and -9 is this author's number against the genre's -6. ⚠ The i–VI family is INFERRED; the research names static i and i–iv. ⚠ **The countermelody reached only 967 distinct answers in 1000 seeds against a floor of 980**, so it was given range: densityRatio 0.45. A counter is combinatorial in the styles it may pick, its density against the melody, and the phrase length it answers inside — one style over a thin density inside a single phrase length has almost nothing a seed can vary.",
